Given state equation is, \[\dot{x}(t)=Ax(t)\] with initial condition \(x(0)\). Since there is no input term, the system is a homogeneous state space system. Let the solution be of the form, \[x(t)=e^{\lambda t}v\] where, \[\lambda ~~\text{is an eigenvalue of matrix \(A\) and}\] \[v~~ \text{is the corresponding eigenvector.}\] Differentiating \(x(t)\), \[\dot{x}(t)
=
\lambda e^{\lambda t}v\] Substituting into the state equation, \[\lambda e^{\lambda t}v
=
Ae^{\lambda t}v\] \[e^{\lambda t}\lambda v
=
e^{\lambda t}Av\] Cancelling the common term \(e^{\lambda t}\), \[Av=\lambda v\] which is the eigenvalue equation. Therefore, for every eigenvalue-eigenvector pair, one solution of the system is, \[x_i(t)=e^{\lambda_i t}v_i\] Since the system is second order and eigenvalues are distinct, there exist two independent solutions, \[x_{1}(t)=e^{\lambda_{1}t}v_{1}\] \[x_{2}(t)=e^{\lambda_{2}t}v_{2}\] By principle of superposition, the complete solution is obtained as a linear combination of these independent solutions. \[x(t)
=
\alpha_{1}e^{\lambda_{1}t}v_{1}
+
\alpha_{2}e^{\lambda_{2}t}v_{2}\] where, \[\alpha_{1}
\quad \text{and} \quad
\alpha_{2} ~~ \text{are constants determined using initial conditions.}\] Writing the above expression in summation form, \[x(t)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{2}
\alpha_i e^{\lambda_i t}v_i\] \[\boxed{\text{(A) }
